`,cnt=`# Plan de Figura Legal para PymesHub en Costa Rica\r
\r
## 1. Proposito\r
\r
Este documento funciona como memo de decision para definir la figura juridica bajo la cual operara PymesHub en Costa Rica. Su objetivo es alinear velocidad de lanzamiento, capacidad de facturacion, gestion de riesgo, imagen comercial, formalizacion bancaria y escalabilidad contractual.\r
\r
## 2. Alcance\r
\r
Este plan cubre:\r
\r
- la figura legal inicial para operar PymesHub;\r
- los criterios para migrar de una figura inicial a una estructura mas robusta;\r
- las validaciones societarias, bancarias y fiscales asociadas;\r
- la asignacion de responsables para formalizacion.\r
\r
## 3. Datos base a completar\r
\r
- Figura inicial seleccionada: \`[POR_DEFINIR_PERSONA_FISICA_O_SOCIEDAD]\`\r
- Fecha objetivo de formalizacion o migracion: \`[FECHA_OBJETIVO]\`\r
- Nombre legal proyectado: \`Otnel\`\r
- Nombre comercial del producto: \`PymesHub\`\r
- Responsable de firma: \`[REPRESENTANTE_LEGAL]\`\r
- Banco objetivo: \`[BANCO_OBJETIVO]\`\r
- Contador o asesoria asignada: \`[CONTADOR_ASIGNADO]\`\r
\r
## 4. Criterios de decision\r
\r
La decision debe considerar, como minimo:\r
\r
- velocidad de salida al mercado;\r
- costo administrativo inicial;\r
- capacidad para emitir comprobantes validos;\r
- separacion patrimonial;\r
- percepcion comercial ante clientes B2B;\r
- facilidad para recibir pagos y abrir cuenta bancaria;\r
- necesidad de socios, inversion o contratacion futura;\r
- gestion de propiedad intelectual y contratos.\r
\r
## 5. Escenario de inicio como persona fisica con actividad lucrativa\r
\r
### Ventajas\r
\r
- menor complejidad de arranque;\r
- menor costo de formalizacion;\r
- posible mayor rapidez para comenzar a facturar;\r
- operacion simple en etapa temprana.\r
\r
### Riesgos\r
\r
- menor separacion patrimonial;\r
- menos robustez frente a clientes corporativos o enterprise;\r
- migracion posterior de contratos, cuentas y comprobantes;\r
- mayor dependencia de la persona titular para firma, cobro y operacion.\r
\r
### Uso recomendado\r
\r
Puede ser una alternativa temporal razonable si:\r
\r
- la etapa es claramente pre-escalamiento;\r
- el volumen contractual es bajo o inicial;\r
- existe validacion del contador sobre la actividad y forma de facturacion;\r
- se deja definida una fecha o gatillo de migracion.\r
\r
## 6. Escenario de inicio o migracion a sociedad\r
\r
### Ventajas\r
\r
- mejor presentacion contractual y comercial;\r
- separacion patrimonial;\r
- mayor facilidad para incorporar socios, inversion, contratos y estructura interna;\r
- mejor soporte para propiedad intelectual, cuentas bancarias empresariales y relaciones con terceros.\r
\r
### Riesgos o costos\r
\r
- mayor costo administrativo inicial;\r
- mayor carga de gobierno y cumplimiento societario;\r
- necesidad de completar tramites y registros adicionales.\r
\r
### Uso recomendado\r
\r
Deberia adoptarse o evaluarse seriamente cuando:\r
\r
- existan contratos enterprise o B2B de mayor entidad;\r
- el riesgo comercial o de datos aumente;\r
- se requiera estructura societaria para socios o inversion;\r
- se quiera profesionalizar la operacion antes de escalar.\r
\r
## 7. Gatillos de migracion recomendados\r
\r
Se recomienda migrar a sociedad si ocurre cualquiera de estos eventos:\r
\r
- firma de contratos relevantes con clientes corporativos;\r
- ingreso de socios o inversion;\r
- contratacion sostenida de personal o contractors;\r
- necesidad de cuenta bancaria empresarial y formalizacion de pagos a terceros;\r
- aumento del riesgo operacional, regulatorio o patrimonial;\r
- necesidad de separar formalmente la propiedad intelectual del producto.\r
\r
## 8. Validaciones externas requeridas\r
\r
Antes de cerrar la decision, deben validarse con asesoria local:\r
\r
- viabilidad de la actividad economica elegida para SaaS y servicios asociados;\r
- tratamiento de facturacion de suscripciones, onboarding y servicios adicionales;\r
- RTBF, si aplica;\r
- impuesto a personas juridicas, si aplica;\r
- requisitos bancarios para cuenta de operacion;\r
- redaccion del objeto social suficiente para PymesHub.\r
\r
## 9. Objeto social sugerido\r
\r
Desarrollo, licenciamiento, comercializacion, implementacion, soporte, mantenimiento y explotacion de plataformas de software, soluciones SaaS, integraciones, automatizacion empresarial, procesamiento documental, analitica operacional y servicios digitales relacionados.\r
\r
## 10. Evidencia y registros\r
\r
Este plan debe conservar o enlazar:\r
\r
- criterio de la asesoria legal o notarial;\r
- criterio del contador;\r
- decision final adoptada;\r
- fecha objetivo de ejecucion;\r
- responsable interno del seguimiento;\r
- evidencia de constitucion, registro o actualizacion cuando se ejecute.\r
\r
## 11. Frecuencia de revision\r
\r
Debe revisarse:\r
\r
- antes del lanzamiento comercial;\r
- cuando cambie la estructura de socios;\r
- al cerrar el primer contrato enterprise;\r
- o al menos una vez al año mientras el placeholder de figura siga abierto.\r
`,unt="# Revision de Marca y Dominio para PymesHub\r\n\r\n## 1. Proposito\r\n\r\nEste documento deja trazabilidad sobre la revision basica del nombre comercial `PymesHub`, sus dominios asociados y la conveniencia de avanzar a registro o a nombre alternativo. Su objetivo es reducir riesgo de conflicto comercial o marcario antes de invertir en contratos, branding o posicionamiento.\r\n\r\n## 2. Datos base\r\n\r\n- Nombre principal: `PymesHub`\r\n- Titular proyectado: `Otnel`\r\n- Jurisdiccion principal de analisis: Costa Rica\r\n- Fecha de revision: `29 de Abril de 2026`\r\n- Responsable: `Lejzer Trana`\r\n\r\n## 3. Dominios a revisar\r\n\r\n- `PymesHub.com`\r\n- `[DOMINIO_ALTERNATIVO_1]`\r\n- `[DOMINIO_ALTERNATIVO_2]`\r\n(No tengo alternativo ni respaldo)\r\n## 4. Alternativas de respaldo\r\n\r\n- `[ALTERNATIVA_1]`\r\n- `[ALTERNATIVA_2]`\r\n- `[ALTERNATIVA_3]`\r\n\r\n## 5. Criterios de revision\r\n\r\nLa revision minima debe considerar:\r\n\r\n- disponibilidad de dominio principal y dominios defensivos;\r\n- busqueda marcaria basica en clases relevantes;\r\n- nombres comerciales o marcas similares que generen riesgo de confusion;\r\n- coincidencias relevantes en redes, handles o productos de software cercanos;\r\n- viabilidad comercial del nombre frente a clientes B2B.\r\n\r\n## 6. Registro de resultados\r\n\r\n| Item | Estado | Fuente o evidencia | Riesgo observado | Decision provisional |\r\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\r\n| Dominio principal | `[PENDIENTE]` | `[EVIDENCIA]` | `[RIESGO]` | `[DECISION]` |\r\n| Dominio alternativo 1 | `[PENDIENTE]` | `[EVIDENCIA]` | `[RIESGO]` | `[DECISION]` |\r\n| Dominio alternativo 2 | `[PENDIENTE]` | `[EVIDENCIA]` | `[RIESGO]` | `[DECISION]` |\r\n| Revision marcaria basica | `[PENDIENTE]` | `[EVIDENCIA]` | `[RIESGO]` | `[DECISION]` |\r\n| Handles/redes | `[PENDIENTE]` | `[EVIDENCIA]` | `[RIESGO]` | `[DECISION]` |\r\n\r\n## 7. Recomendacion de trabajo\r\n\r\nSi PymesHub avanza a ventas formales y contratos recurrentes, conviene:\r\n\r\n- confirmar pronto si `PymesHub` sera el nombre definitivo;\r\n- asegurar el dominio principal;\r\n- evaluar registro marcario cuando la entidad titular y el mercado objetivo esten claros;\r\n- mantener alternativas listas por si se detecta conflicto relevante.\r\n",dnt="# PymesHub — Product overview\n\n- [English](#english)\n- [Español](#espanol)\n\n---\n\n## English\n\n**PymesHub is a local-commerce and delivery marketplace for Costa Rica.** A customer orders from a nearby business; the business prepares the order; the order is picked up at the counter or delivered by a courier. PymesHub is the software that connects the three — it does not sell the products, does not provide the transport, and does not process a card payment.\n\nThis page describes the product as it exists in the current repository. It is a public reference, not a certification of availability, and it deliberately leaves out the retired SaaS-era documentation (team inbox, CRM, OCR, WhatsApp/Telegram assistants, workspaces) that no longer describes this marketplace.\n\n### Who uses it\n\n| Audience | What they do | Surface |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Customer | Finds nearby businesses, orders, tracks the order, keeps favourites, writes reviews | Web (`pymeshub.lat`) and the mobile app |\n| Business | Publishes a catalogue, works a live order board, answers reviews, manages staff and locations | Web dashboard and the mobile app |\n| Courier | Accepts delivery offers, shares location during a delivery, gets paid for the run | Mobile app |\n| Platform operator | Administration: businesses, orders, categories, payouts and an audit log | Admin area of the web app |\n\n### How money works\n\n- The customer pays **the business** for the products and **the courier** for the delivery.\n- PymesHub charges the business a **flat fee** for the app. There is **no per-order commission** and no share of the customer's payment, and the platform never handles the customer's money — which is why there is no payment gateway in the flow.\n- The enabled payment methods are **cash on delivery** (`CASH`) and **SINPE Móvil** (`SINPE_MOVIL`). `CARD` exists in the data model but is not enabled.\n- Payment is a **separate axis from fulfilment**: an order can be completed and paid in the same moment (cash) or paid before the business accepts (SINPE).\n\nSource: `packages/shared/src/plans.ts`, `packages/shared/src/order-state.ts`.\n\n### Plans and prices\n\nPrices are in **colones (CRC)** and include Costa Rican **IVA (13%)**, which the platform absorbs and remits. The launch price book is `launch-2026`. There are five tiers and two cadences (`MONTHLY`, `YEARLY`); the annual price is **ten months** of the monthly price.\n\n| Plan | Monthly | Yearly |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| FREE | ₡0 | ₡0 |\n| EMPRENDE | ₡10 900 | ₡109 000 |\n| STARTER | ₡19 900 | ₡199 000 |\n| GROWTH | ₡29 900 | ₡299 000 |\n| BUSINESS | ₡59 900 | ₡599 000 |\n\n**Limits depend on the tier alone** — an annual subscription has exactly the same numbers as a monthly one:\n\n| Limit | FREE | EMPRENDE | STARTER | GROWTH | BUSINESS |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Locations | 1 | 1 | 1 | 2 | 5 |\n| Staff accounts | 1 | 2 | 3 | 5 | 15 |\n| Products | 15 | 60 | 250 | 600 | 1 000 |\n| Option groups per product | 1 | 2 | 4 | 5 | 6 |\n| Options per group | 5 | 8 | 12 | 16 | 20 |\n| Images per product | 2 | 3 | 6 | 8 | 10 |\n| Storage | 150 MB | 600 MB | 3 GB | 6 GB | 10 GB |\n| Active promotions | 0 | 2 | 8 | 15 | 25 |\n| Analytics window (days) | 30 | 90 | 730 | 1 095 | 1 095 |\n| Inventory tracking | No | No | Yes | Yes | Yes |\n| Express orders accepted per day | 10 | 30 | 90 | 250 | Unlimited |\n\n- Raising a price is a **new price book**, not a release: a subscription keeps the book it joined under, so an increase never reaches a business that is already in.\n- `FREE` is **permanent**, not a trial, and is never charged.\n\n### Subscription states\n\n| Status | Access | Listed in the marketplace |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `ACTIVE` | Full plan limits | Yes |\n| `GRACE` (30 days) | Full plan limits | Yes |\n| `PAST_DUE` | Falls back to `FREE` limits | Yes |\n| `SUSPENDED` (after 90 days) | Falls back to `FREE` limits | No |\n\nThe status is **derived from the subscription's dates**, not read from a stored column, so it is correct whenever it is asked.\n\n**Never limited, on any plan:** taking and advancing orders, shop hours and pausing a location, reviews in both directions, seeing payouts, and deleting the account.\n\nSource: `packages/shared/src/plans.ts`.\n\n### The life of an order\n\nStatuses: `PENDING → ACCEPTED → PREPARING → READY → OUT_FOR_DELIVERY → COMPLETED`, plus the terminal `CANCELLED` and `REJECTED`.\n\n- **Fulfilment** is `PICKUP` or `DELIVERY` — a separate question from speed.\n- **Speed** is `STANDARD` or `EXPRESS`.\n- **Actors** are `CUSTOMER`, `BUSINESS`, `COURIER`, `ADMIN` and `SYSTEM`. A transition is legal only for the actor it belongs to, and there is only one transition table, so the API and every screen agree.\n- A `PICKUP` order is completed at the counter; only a `DELIVERY` goes out the door.\n- Payment methods: `CASH`, `SINPE_MOVIL` (and `CARD`, not enabled). Payment statuses: `UNPAID`, `PENDING`, `PAID`, `REFUNDED`, `FAILED`.\n\nSource: `packages/shared/src/order-state.ts`.\n\n### Who can do what (roles)\n\nA business has four membership roles: **`OWNER`**, **`MANAGER`**, **`STAFF`** and **`COURIER`**. Staff invitations cover `OWNER`, `MANAGER` and `STAFF`; a courier is added to a business by email and does not go through a staff invitation.\n\nSource: `packages/shared/src/schemas/user.ts`, `packages/shared/src/schemas/affiliation.ts`.\n\n### Platform and architecture\n\n- **One backend.** A single **Cloudflare Worker** (`pymeshub-api`, Hono 4 + tRPC 11) is the only thing that writes to the database.\n- **Data.** Cloudflare **D1** (SQLite; production database `pymhubdb`, staging `pymeshub-staging`) through **Drizzle ORM**; **KV** for cache and rate limiting; **R2** for media (`pymeshub-media`, private, served through `/files/:id`); **Queues** (`pymeshub-order-events` with a dead-letter queue) and **Durable Objects** (`OrderRoom`, `RateLimitRoom`) for live order updates.\n- **Auth.** Better Auth.\n- **Clients.** Web is React 19 + Vite 7 + Tailwind + Radix/shadcn + wouter + TanStack Query; mobile is Expo 57 + React Native + expo-router; desktop is Tauri 2.\n- **Hosts.** Web at `pymeshub.lat`, API at `api.pymeshub.lat`.\n- **Monorepo.** pnpm 10 + Turbo, languages **es / en**, and money stored as an integer in the currency's minor unit (CRC has no minor unit).\n- **Legacy.** `apps/api` (NestJS + Prisma) is retired SaaS code and is **not** the marketplace API.\n\nSource: `README.md`, `wrangler.toml`, `packages/trpc-api/wrangler.toml`, `packages/shared/src/money.ts`.\n\n### What the API exposes\n\nThe marketplace API is organized into these tRPC routers:\n\n`admin`, `affiliations`, `business`, `businesses`, `cart`, `catalog`, `couriers`, `crash-report`, `deliveries`, `devices`, `favorites`, `health`, `idea`, `identity`, `notifications`, `orders`, `products`, `promotions`, `reviews`, `subscription`, `support`, `uploads`, `users`.\n\nSource: `packages/trpc-api/src/routers`.\n\n### Repository, licence and contact\n\n- Licence: **MIT** (`LICENSE`).\n- General contact: **hola@pymeshub.com** · Privacy: **privacidad@pymeshub.lat** · Accessibility: **accesibilidad@pymeshub.lat**.\n\n### What this page does not claim\n\n- It describes the repository; it is not a statement that every flow is deployed or verified in production.\n- No online card payment is offered, and there is no per-order commission.\n- Features from the retired SaaS era (team inbox, CRM, OCR, WhatsApp/Telegram assistants, workspaces) are not part of this product.\n\n---\n\n## Español\n\n**PymesHub es un marketplace de comercio local y entregas para Costa Rica.** Un cliente pide a un negocio cercano; el negocio prepara el pedido; el pedido se retira en el local o lo entrega un repartidor. PymesHub es el software que conecta a los tres: no vende los productos, no presta el transporte y no procesa el pago con tarjeta.\n\nEsta página describe el producto tal como existe en el repositorio actual. Es una referencia pública, no una certificación de disponibilidad, y deja fuera a propósito la documentación retirada de la etapa SaaS (inbox de equipo, CRM, OCR, asistentes de WhatsApp/Telegram, workspaces), que ya no describe este marketplace.\n\n### Para quién es\n\n| Audiencia | Qué hace | Superficie |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Cliente | Busca negocios cercanos, pide, sigue el pedido, guarda favoritos y escribe reseñas | Web (`pymeshub.lat`) y la app móvil |\n| Negocio | Publica su catálogo, trabaja el tablero de pedidos, responde reseñas y gestiona equipo y sucursales | Panel web y app móvil |\n| Repartidor | Acepta ofertas de entrega, comparte su ubicación durante la entrega y cobra por el viaje | App móvil |\n| Operador de la plataforma | Administración: negocios, pedidos, categorías, pagos y un registro de auditoría | Área de admin del panel web |\n\n### Cómo funciona el dinero\n\n- El cliente le paga **al negocio** por los productos y **al repartidor** por la entrega.\n- PymesHub le cobra al negocio una **tarifa fija** por la app. **No hay comisión por pedido** ni reparto del pago del cliente, y la plataforma nunca toca ese dinero; por eso no hay una pasarela de pago en el flujo.\n- Los métodos de pago habilitados son **efectivo contra entrega** (`CASH`) y **SINPE Móvil** (`SINPE_MOVIL`). `CARD` existe en el modelo de datos, pero no está habilitado.\n- El pago es un **eje aparte de la entrega**: un pedido puede completarse y pagarse en el mismo momento (efectivo) o pagarse antes de que el negocio acepte (SINPE).\n\nFuente: `packages/shared/src/plans.ts`, `packages/shared/src/order-state.ts`.\n\n### Planes y precios\n\nLos precios están en **colones (CRC)** e incluyen el **IVA (13%)** de Costa Rica, que la plataforma absorbe y entera. El libro de precios de lanzamiento es `launch-2026`. Hay cinco niveles y dos periodicidades (`MONTHLY`, `YEARLY`); el precio anual es **diez meses** del mensual.\n\n| Plan | Mensual | Anual |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| FREE | ₡0 | ₡0 |\n| EMPRENDE | ₡10 900 | ₡109 000 |\n| STARTER | ₡19 900 | ₡199 000 |\n| GROWTH | ₡29 900 | ₡299 000 |\n| BUSINESS | ₡59 900 | ₡599 000 |\n\n**Los límites dependen solo del nivel** — un plan anual tiene exactamente los mismos números que uno mensual:\n\n| Límite | FREE | EMPRENDE | STARTER | GROWTH | BUSINESS |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Sucursales | 1 | 1 | 1 | 2 | 5 |\n| Cuentas de staff | 1 | 2 | 3 | 5 | 15 |\n| Productos | 15 | 60 | 250 | 600 | 1 000 |\n| Grupos de opciones por producto | 1 | 2 | 4 | 5 | 6 |\n| Opciones por grupo | 5 | 8 | 12 | 16 | 20 |\n| Imágenes por producto | 2 | 3 | 6 | 8 | 10 |\n| Almacenamiento | 150 MB | 600 MB | 3 GB | 6 GB | 10 GB |\n| Promociones activas | 0 | 2 | 8 | 15 | 25 |\n| Ventana de analítica (días) | 30 | 90 | 730 | 1 095 | 1 095 |\n| Control de inventario | No | No | Sí | Sí | Sí |\n| Express aceptados por día | 10 | 30 | 90 | 250 | Sin límite |\n\n- Subir un precio es un **libro de precios nuevo**, no un despliegue: la suscripción conserva el libro con el que entró, así que un aumento nunca alcanza a un negocio que ya está dentro.\n- `FREE` es **permanente**, no una prueba, y nunca se cobra.\n\n### Estados de la suscripción\n\n| Estado | Acceso | Aparece en el marketplace |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `ACTIVE` | Límites completos del plan | Sí |\n| `GRACE` (30 días) | Límites completos del plan | Sí |\n| `PAST_DUE` | Baja a los límites de `FREE` | Sí |\n| `SUSPENDED` (a los 90 días) | Baja a los límites de `FREE` | No |\n\nEl estado se **deriva de las fechas** de la suscripción, no se lee de una columna guardada, así que es correcto en el momento en que se pregunta.\n\n**Nunca se limita, en ningún plan:** tomar y avanzar pedidos, horarios y pausar el local, reseñas en ambas direcciones, ver pagos y eliminar la cuenta.\n\nFuente: `packages/shared/src/plans.ts`.\n\n### La vida de un pedido\n\nEstados: `PENDING → ACCEPTED → PREPARING → READY → OUT_FOR_DELIVERY → COMPLETED`, más los terminales `CANCELLED` y `REJECTED`.\n\n- La **forma de entrega** es `PICKUP` o `DELIVERY`, una pregunta aparte de la velocidad.\n- La **velocidad** es `STANDARD` o `EXPRESS`.\n- Los **actores** son `CUSTOMER`, `BUSINESS`, `COURIER`, `ADMIN` y `SYSTEM`. Cada transición es legal solo para el actor al que pertenece, y existe una sola tabla de transiciones, así que la API y cada pantalla coinciden.\n- Un pedido `PICKUP` se completa en el mostrador; solo un `DELIVERY` sale a la calle.\n- Métodos de pago: `CASH`, `SINPE_MOVIL` (y `CARD`, no habilitado). Estados de pago: `UNPAID`, `PENDING`, `PAID`, `REFUNDED`, `FAILED`.\n\nFuente: `packages/shared/src/order-state.ts`.\n\n### Quién puede hacer qué (roles)\n\nUn negocio tiene cuatro roles de membresía: **`OWNER`**, **`MANAGER`**, **`STAFF`** y **`COURIER`**. Las invitaciones de staff cubren `OWNER`, `MANAGER` y `STAFF`; a un repartidor se lo suma al negocio por correo y no pasa por una invitación de staff.\n\nFuente: `packages/shared/src/schemas/user.ts`, `packages/shared/src/schemas/affiliation.ts`.\n\n### Plataforma y arquitectura\n\n- **Un solo backend.** Un único **Cloudflare Worker** (`pymeshub-api`, Hono 4 + tRPC 11) es lo único que escribe en la base de datos.\n- **Datos.** Cloudflare **D1** (SQLite; base de producción `pymhubdb`, staging `pymeshub-staging`) con **Drizzle ORM**; **KV** para caché y límites de tasa; **R2** para media (`pymeshub-media`, privado, servido por `/files/:id`); **Queues** (`pymeshub-order-events` con cola de mensajes muertos) y **Durable Objects** (`OrderRoom`, `RateLimitRoom`) para las actualizaciones en vivo del pedido.\n- **Auth.** Better Auth.\n- **Clientes.** La web es React 19 + Vite 7 + Tailwind + Radix/shadcn + wouter + TanStack Query; el móvil es Expo 57 + React Native + expo-router; el escritorio es Tauri 2.\n- **Hosts.** Web en `pymeshub.lat`, API en `api.pymeshub.lat`.\n- **Monorepo.** pnpm 10 + Turbo, idiomas **es / en**, y el dinero guardado como entero en la unidad menor de la moneda (el colón no tiene unidad menor).\n- **Legado.** `apps/api` (NestJS + Prisma) es código SaaS retirado y **no** es la API del marketplace.\n\nFuente: `README.md`, `wrangler.toml`, `packages/trpc-api/wrangler.toml`, `packages/shared/src/money.ts`.\n\n### Qué expone la API\n\nLa API del marketplace se organiza en estos routers de tRPC:\n\n`admin`, `affiliations`, `business`, `businesses`, `cart`, `catalog`, `couriers`, `crash-report`, `deliveries`, `devices`, `favorites`, `health`, `idea`, `identity`, `notifications`, `orders`, `products`, `promotions`, `reviews`, `subscription`, `support`, `uploads`, `users`.\n\nFuente: `packages/trpc-api/src/routers`.\n\n### Repositorio, licencia y contacto\n\n- Licencia: **MIT** (`LICENSE`).\n- Contacto general: **hola@pymeshub.com** · Privacidad: **privacidad@pymeshub.lat** · Accesibilidad: **accesibilidad@pymeshub.lat**.\n\n### Lo que esta página no afirma\n\n- Describe el repositorio; no afirma que cada flujo esté desplegado ni verificado en producción.\n- No se ofrece pago con tarjeta en línea y no hay comisión por pedido.\n- Las funciones de la etapa SaaS retirada (inbox de equipo, CRM, OCR, asistentes de WhatsApp/Telegram, workspaces) no son parte de este producto.\n",pnt=`# PymesHub — Getting started

- [English](#english)
- [Español](#espanol)

---

## English

**Three short paths: buying, selling, and delivering.** Each step below is something the product actually asks for, in the order it asks.

### Before you start

| You are | You need |
| --- | --- |
| Buying | A phone with the app (iOS or Android) or a browser, and an email address. You must be 18 or older. |
| Selling | Your shop's name and address, its opening hours, and a decision about pickup, delivery or both. Your currency is chosen once, at the beginning. |
| Delivering | A phone with the app. A vehicle is described in your profile but the platform does not require you to declare one to be reviewed. |

### Buying

1. **Create an account.** Name, email, phone. You confirm you are 18 or older and accept the terms, and both confirmations are recorded against the account.
2. **Find a shop.** Search by name or product, open a category, or look at what is near you. You can limit the list to shops that are open right now, or that deliver.
3. **Build a cart.** A cart belongs to a single business. Adding something from another business either starts a fresh cart or asks you to replace the one you have. A line holds up to 20 units and a cart up to 40 lines, and you can add a note to a line (up to 300 characters).
4. **Choose how it arrives.** **Pickup** at the counter, or **delivery** to an address — a delivery needs a map pin, and the address must be one you saved. Then choose **standard** or **express** where the shop offers it.
5. **Apply a coupon** if you have one. If a code does not work, the cart tells you why: not found, not yet valid, expired, out of uses, or your order is below its minimum.
6. **Check out.** The total is calculated by the server, so the number you see is the number the order has. Pay **cash on delivery** or by **SINPE Móvil**; card is not enabled.
7. **Follow it.** The order moves through its states, and while it is out for delivery you can see the courier's latest position.
8. **Afterwards.** Leave a review (one per order, 1–5 stars, up to four photos), reply to nothing — the business may reply to you — save the shop as a favourite, and reorder it in one tap. A reorder is additive: it adds the items again.

### Selling

1. **Create the business.** Name, category and address. The **category is required** and must be a specific one, not a broad group. **Currency is fixed here and never changes**, because a shop that switched currency would have to re-price every product.
2. **Fill in the shop.** Description, logo and cover, phone and email, and then **hours**: per day of the week, with as many windows as the day needs (a lunch break is a gap between two windows).
3. **Decide how you serve.** Whether you deliver, whether you offer pickup, your delivery radius, your delivery fee, your estimated preparation time and your minimum order. You can also choose to **cover the delivery fee** yourself, so the customer pays nothing for it.
4. **Load the catalogue.** Products with prices, option groups (sizes, extras), images and stock. Inventory tracking is available from the \`STARTER\` plan up. Promotions are percentage, fixed amount or free delivery, with a minimum, a validity window and a usage limit.
5. **Invite your team.** Owners, managers and staff are invited by email, and the invitation lasts **14 days**. A courier is added differently: you search verified courier profiles and invite one, and it becomes a member only when that person accepts.
6. **Ask for the verified badge** (optional). Legal name, tax identification, support email, a domain and supporting documents. What you send is private and is never handed back through a public read.
7. **Go live.** A shop is \`DRAFT\` while you set it up; customers see \`ACTIVE\` and \`CLOSED\` shops only, so switch to \`ACTIVE\` when the catalogue is ready.
8. **Work the orders.** Accept or reject each new order, move it through preparation, hand it to a courier or deliver it yourself, and complete it. With cash on delivery you collect; with SINPE you confirm the transfer against the proof.
9. **Watch the numbers.** Orders today, completed revenue for today (separate from all-time), alerts that need attention, and insights such as your top product and average preparation time.
10. **Pause instead of closing.** If the kitchen is full, a branch can be paused for 15, 30 or 60 minutes, or until you resume it — the shop stays open, the customers are told to come back later.

### Delivering

1. **Create an account and fill a courier profile.** Display name, service area, an optional work zone with a radius, a short bio, your vehicle's details and whether you are available.
2. **Get verified.** Your profile is reviewed: \`PENDING\`, \`VERIFIED\` or \`REJECTED\`. Only a verified profile can be found by a business, and only your own acceptance makes you a member of one.
3. **Keep your position fresh when you want work.** Offers are made to couriers who are near the pickup, so what counts is where your device is, not where your profile says you work. A work zone is never treated as a position.
4. **Take an offer.** Offers carry the pickup, the drop-off area, the distance to the pickup, your fee, the customer's delivery fee and when the offer expires — **two minutes**.
5. **Run it.** Four steps: start towards the pickup, arrive, confirm the pickup, complete. Your location is shared with the customer and the business only while the run is active, and the server keeps only the latest point.
6. **Get paid.** Your payout is the delivery fee quoted from the route. On a **cash** order you collect the whole total at the door and give the business its part; on **SINPE** you are paid for the delivery.
7. **Rate and be rated.** Both directions, 1–5 stars. Your own profile shows your completed runs and your rating once you have one.

### If something does not go as planned

- The business did not accept your order: you can cancel it at no cost.
- An item ran out: the business can cancel the line or the order, and you are told why.
- You are below the minimum: the cart tells you exactly how much is missing.
- The delivery could not be completed: you and the business agree on another attempt, or the order is cancelled with a reason.
- Anything else: the [support policy](/documentation/support-policy) says where to write, and the [FAQ](/documentation/faq) answers the common cases.

### What this page does not claim

- It is a walkthrough of the product as it behaves today, not a tutorial with screenshots, and not a substitute for the Legal Center documents you accept when you create an account.
- A step that depends on your plan is marked as such; the full table is on the [plans and billing](/documentation/plans-and-billing) page.

